<% content_for :title, "yrby — Rails as a Yjs backend" %>

<section class="pt-6 pb-16 sm:pt-14">
  <p class="mb-4 font-mono text-sm text-accent">gem install yrby-rails</p>
  <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-5xl">
    Rails as a real Yjs backend.
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-zinc-400">
    yrby binds <a class="text-zinc-200 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent" href="https://github.com/y-crdt/y-crdt">y-crdt</a>,
    the Rust engine behind Y.js, into Ruby — and builds the rest of the stack
    around it: a sync server for Action Cable and AnyCable, a browser provider,
    and server-side reading and rendering of the documents. Real-time
    collaboration with no Node process anywhere in the path.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <%= link_to "Read the docs", doc_path("getting-started"),
                class: "rounded-lg bg-accent-strong px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-rose-500" %>
    <%= link_to "Try the demos", demos_path,
                class: "rounded-lg border border-zinc-700 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-200 hover:border-zinc-500 hover:text-white" %>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-500">
      The whole server side of a collaborative document is one channel:
    </p>
    <%= code_block "ruby", <<~RUBY %>
      class DocumentChannel < ApplicationCable::Channel
        include Y::ActionCable

        on_load   { |key|         Y::Document.load_state(key) }
        on_change { |key, update| Y::Document.append(key, update) }

        def subscribed    = sync_subscribed(params[:id])
        def receive(data) = sync_receive(data, params[:id])
      end
    RUBY
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-6 text-zinc-400">
      Every update is recorded through <code>on_change</code> before it is
      acked or broadcast to anyone. Replaying your store always rebuilds the
      document, on one process or a hundred.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">
    Any Yjs shape, no editor required
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    The server treats a document as opaque CRDT state, so the same channel syncs
    a rich text editor, a spreadsheet, a whiteboard, or anything else with a Yjs
    binding. Each demo below runs against this site, on the store described in
    <%= link_to "Storage", doc_path("storage"), class: "text-accent underline decoration-rose-400/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-rose-400" %>.
  </p>
  <ul class="demo-list">
    <% Demos::ALL.each do |demo| %>
      <li>
        <%= link_to demo.title, demo_path(demo.slug) %>
        <code><%= demo.shape %></code>
        <span><%= demo.blurb %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">This site</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    The site you are reading is a Rails app in the yrby repo
    (<a class="text-accent underline decoration-rose-400/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-rose-400" href="https://github.com/jpcamara/yrby/tree/main/site"><code>site/</code></a>).
    Its demo rooms run the canonical stack these docs teach —
    <code>Y::Document.load_state</code> / <code>Y::Document.append</code> on
    SQLite — behind the channel's two hooks. Its README covers the store, the
    throttle design, and what it costs to host.
  </p>
</section>
